        {
          "path": "_m/s/f/e/8/obelisk-of-axum-wp/celebrate-the-return-of-axum-o.jpg",
          "filename": "Celebrate The Return of Axum Obelisk (3170898463).jpg",
          "caption": "This is the ultimate \"deal toy.\"  It was on display in the lobby of our hotel in Axum, the Ramhai Hotel.  As the label indicates (see the close-up below), the object  celebrates the return to Axum of an obelisk -  an ancient funerary monument dating to the Axumite Kingdom -  looted by the Italian government in the 30s.\nAccording to Wikipedia,\n\"The Obelisk of Axum is a 1700-year-old, 24-meters (78-foot) tall granite obelisk, weighing 160 tonnes. It is decorated with two false doors at the base, and decorations resembling windows on all sides. The obelisk ends in a semicircular top part, which used to be enclosed by metal frames.\"   It was probably erected in the 4th century of the common era.  en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obelisk_of_Axum\nIf the Italian government couldn't keep Ethiopia, which it unsuccessfully attempted to colonize in the late 30s, at least it managed to hang on to a piece of stolen Ethiopian history - the Axum Obelisk - much longer than many would have liked.\nWhen Italian forces reached Axum in 1935, they found the Axum Obelisk in a ruined state, having been toppled and broken for centuries, perhaps longer.\nIn keeping with the Imperial Roman tradition of hauling loot from conquered nations to the capital city, Rome, the Italians, under official orders, cut the fallen obelisk into six pieces, hauled the sections overland manually to the Eritrean port of Massawa and loaded it onto a boat bound for Naples.\nThe reconstructed Axum Obelisk was raised in front of the Ministry for Italian Africa ( ! ) in Rome, across from the Circus Maximus, in October 1937.\nIn 1947, having lost both Ethiopia and WWII, Italy agreed to return the Axum Obelisk to Ethiopia.  A series of complicated delays then ensued, including claims that Ethiopia had relinquished its claim to the Obelisk.  At last, in 1997, the Italian President of the moment agreed to repatriate the Obelisk.\nThe first steps toward dismantling the Obelisk took place in Rome in 2003.  To make a very long story short, the obelisk returned to Ethiopia in 2005; reassembly of the Obelisk in Axum began in June 2008; and the restored Axum Obelisk was unveiled in September of the same year.\nFor much more information about the Axum Obelisk, visit Wikipedia at:\n\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obelisk_of_Axum",
          "alt": "This is the ultimate \"deal toy.\"  It was on display in the lobby of our hotel in Axum, the Ramhai Hotel.  As the label indicates (see the close-up below), the object  celebrates the return to Axum of an obelisk -  an ancient funerary monument dating to the Axumite Kingdom -  looted by the Italian government in the 30s.\nAccording to Wikipedia,\n\"The Obelisk of Axum is a 1700-year-old, 24-meters (78-foot) tall granite obelisk, weighing 160 tonnes. It is decorated with two false doors at the base, and decorations resembling windows on all sides. The obelisk ends in a semicircular top part, which used to be enclosed by metal frames.\"   It was probably erected in the 4th century of the common era.  en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obelisk_of_Axum\nIf the Italian government couldn't keep Ethiopia, which it unsuccessfully attempted to colonize in the late 30s, at least it managed to hang on to a piece of stolen Ethiopian history - the Axum Obelisk - much longer than many would have liked.\nWhen Italian forces reached Axum in 1935, they found the Axum Obelisk in a ruined state, having been toppled and broken for centuries, perhaps longer.\nIn keeping with the Imperial Roman tradition of hauling loot from conquered nations to the capital city, Rome, the Italians, under official orders, cut the fallen obelisk into six pieces, hauled the sections overland manually to the Eritrean port of Massawa and loaded it onto a boat bound for Naples.\nThe reconstructed Axum Obelisk was raised in front of the Ministry for Italian Africa ( ! ) in Rome, across from the Circus Maximus, in October 1937.\nIn 1947, having lost both Ethiopia and WWII, Italy agreed to return the Axum Obelisk to Ethiopia.  A series of complicated delays then ensued, including claims that Ethiopia had relinquished its claim to the Obelisk.  At last, in 1997, the Italian President of the moment agreed to repatriate the Obelisk.\nThe first steps toward dismantling the Obelisk took place in Rome in 2003.  To make a very long story short, the obelisk returned to Ethiopia in 2005; reassembly of the Obelisk in Axum began in June 2008; and the restored Axum Obelisk was unveiled in September of the same year.\nFor much more information about the Axum Obelisk, visit Wikipedia at:\n\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obelisk_of_Axum",
          "width": 1231,
          "height": 1691,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY 2.0",
          "artist": "A. Davey from Where I Live Now: Pacific Northwest",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Celebrate_The_Return_of_Axum_Obelisk_(3170898463).jpg",
          "sourceType": "commons-category",
          "sourceArticleId": "obelisk-of-axum-wp",
          "sourceGeohash4": "sfe8",
